WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.600
Good day, everyone, and happy New Year.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.979
I'm Jack Van Horn from the University of Southern
California.

00:00:07.979 --> 00:00:11.160
And I'd
like to welcome you to the next webinar on

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.900
big data to knowledge, guide to the fundamentals
of data science.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.060
We're absolutely delighted to have Dr. Ian
Foster from the University of Chicago and

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.760
Argonne National
Laboratories here to discuss why the cloud

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.260
matters for data science.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:30.060
Dr. Foster is a well-regarded computer
scientist based at the University of Chicago,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.470
where he is a professor of computer science.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.930
And he's also the Director of Data Science
in the data science and learning division

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.050
and distinguished fellow at
Argonne National Laboratory.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.610
He received his bachelor's degree from the
University of Canterbury in New

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:49.160
Zealand, and his PhD from Imperial College
in London, England-- both for computer science.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.309
And is well known
for his work on the developments of grid computing,

00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:58.820
of the Globus network, of how to move data
around efficiently

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.039
on the internet.

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:03.850
And his work involves developing tools and
techniques which allow people to use high

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.500
performance computing technologies to do quantitative
things new and easily.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.250
We really like to welcome Dr. Foster, and
I'd like to welcome you to the audience to

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.439
text in and submit your
questions via the little questions submission

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:20.740
system here.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.630
And I will work to gather those up and ask
them on your

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.540
behalf of Dr. Foster towards the end of the
hour.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.830
Without further ado though, I'd like to turn
the webinar over to Dr.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.990
Foster to tell us why the clock matters for
data science.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.290
Ian, the stage is yours.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.180
Thank you, Jack.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.070
Good morning.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.799
Good afternoon for those on the East Coast.

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:47.960
Thanks for joining.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:54.320
So what I want
to do today is talk to you about cloud and

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:02.460
hopefully tell you a few things that you don't
know about it and give you

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.640
an idea of why it is important.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:14.580
I think a very fundamentally transformative
technology that we should all be deeply

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.880
familiar with.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:25.860
And in some cases, working how to apply it
in our science.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:37.470
So my first slide here quotes a few interesting
assertions by the Gartner Group about cloud

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:38.470
computing.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.490
This is
from a fairly recent report of theirs.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.840
And it has some interesting observations.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:52.390
The first bullet there asserts that
many large enterprises will be moving to largely

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.190
cloud only strategies for new IT.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:03:00.330
In other words, they'll be
deploying new data systems, computing systems,

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.810
and so forth not in-house, but on resources
provided by

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.560
companies like Amazon or Google or Microsoft.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.240
Increasingly, those applications are going
to be mission-critical.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:23.010
That they're going to be getting more tech
specific, moving towards using a container

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.790
rather than virtual machines to host things
and well say a little bit about

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.240
that later.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:37.640
That they'll increasingly be using AI technologies
and learning and data science technologies

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.640
that will
increasingly be cloud hosted.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:48.720
And starting to use a technology called cloud-native
serverless computing which I'll say a bit

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.750
more about as well
because I think it's significant when we think

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.600
about how we might create data science applications
that we intend

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.880
to scale to large numbers of users.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:07.540
So let's sort of-- just by way of background--
now, let's move on and spend a

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:15.120
bit of time talking about what cloud computing
is and how one might be able to use it.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:21.090
So first of all, perhaps though, I'll just
spend a minute saying, well, why is it that

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:25.050
cloud computing is able to do all
these wonderful things?

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:31.660
Why is it that people, at least in enterprises,
are looking so seriously at running large

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.350
and
complex applications on cloud computing.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:39.820
Well, first of all, it's due to the economies
of scale that are inherent in

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:48.740
the extremely large deployments that are operated
by a cloud provider.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:55.390
So this is a Google machine room I think near
Chicago.

00:04:55.390 --> 00:05:00.520
But there are many of these warehouse scale
computing

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:06.710
systems being operated by a growing number
of companies.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.790
And well, it's sort of a fascinating picture.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:14.560
You can
see the enormous sizes that they are able

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.720
to operate and, therefore, that translates
into certainly issues of

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:27.830
scalability-- economies of scale in terms
of operations, also reliability.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:32.760
And obviously perhaps, also has implications
for things like security.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:39.720
You've got very dedicated professional staff
operating and ensuring the security of the

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:40.750
sort of systems.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.240
So, arguably, cloud computing systems are
more

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:48.370
secure than whatever might be operated by
your home institution.

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:55.990
When people use the word cloud, I'll just
mention briefly, to refer to a variety of

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:57.210
different technologies.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.220
You'll hear
people talking about private clouds or academic,

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:02.000
or community clouds.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:10.790
These tend to be clusters operated inside
a machine room in an institution running certain

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:19.310
software that will allow access to certain
storage models and

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:21.370
computing models.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.530
But mostly I'm going to be talking today about
public cloud, which is the sorts of systems

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.670
that I just showed you a
picture of.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.220
And I think that's an important distinction.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:35.330
Public cloud tends to have a far richer set
of services.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.900
And it's
those services that I think are really part

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.340
of what makes cloud computing exciting.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:47.270
So let's listen a minute now talking about
what the cloud is.

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:51.260
So, in my view, you can think of cloud computing
as

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:56.190
providing three different sets of capabilities
to users.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:07:01.930
As One that probably many of us are familiar
with is you can

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.610
think of the cloud as a virtual computer.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:11.310
It's something that lets you run a virtual
machine, will store a file-- run

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:14.870
many virtual machine, store many files.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:21.340
Just the things you do on your own on your
workstation or institutional computer, but

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:26.320
doing it in the machine room
like the one that I just showed you.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:31.120
And of course that has certain benefits.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:39.240
We can, for example, deploy a virtual
machine and run an application that perhaps

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.639
requires more resources than we have available
locally.

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:49.750
Perhaps that uses a specialized hardware device
like a graphical processing unit, GPU, that

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.419
we don't have
available locally.

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:56.169
But it's really not fundamentally different
from what we do in our ordinary, everyday

00:07:56.169 --> 00:07:58.430
computing.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:09.010
The second model of cloud computing is as
what you might call a lab assistant.

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:18.360
It's a set of software services that does
things that we could do ourselves locally

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:25.230
but is perhaps or more efficient to
do remotely.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:29.800
We all make use of the sort of software as
a service side of cloud computing everyday

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:37.460
if we use
Google Docs or Google mail or some travel

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:39.590
planning service and so on and so forth.

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:44.380
And there are a growing
number also of more scientific software as

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:46.950
a service offerings available.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:49.330
We'll cover some of those a bit later.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:55.510
And then thirdly, and this is perhaps the
most interesting part of cloud computing,

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:59.370
is it's increasingly as a
programming platform.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:07.130
It's a set of platform services-- and we'll
go through what some of those are a little

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:12.450
bit later-
- with very rich and powerful platform services

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:20.810
that we can use to build our own very interesting
applications often

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:26.810
more quickly, more reliably, more securely
than we might be able to do if we built them

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:27.810
locally.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:36.960
And we might run those applications on the
cloud itself as I sort of show in the lower

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:37.960
right here.

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:42.570
Or we might build
applications that run in our lab but that

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:47.111
happen to use some of these platform services
to, for example, manage

00:09:47.111 --> 00:09:51.180
workflows or store results of computations.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.570
Those hybrid sorts of things are often very
interesting.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:10:02.700
Now, so here, just to give you an idea of--
when I say that there's a lot of platform

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:09.760
services, this is a figure that
shows the platform services provided by one

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:14.320
particular cloud vendor, Amazon Web Services.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:20.380
And I won't go
through them here in any detail, but certainly

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:23.750
you'll get the idea that there's a lot of
them.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:32.070
You'll see they cover the topics compute and
storage certainly, but also other areas like

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:37.730
content delivery, workflow
management, notification services, monitoring.

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:43.670
In fact, this is only a subset of all the
services that Amazon

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:44.670
provides.

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:49.920
And people are starting to use these sorts
of services to write some very interesting

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:51.260
data science
applications.

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:58.890
And I may have time to say a bit about those
later.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:06.540
One thing that this emergence of this new
programming platform if you like has spurred

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:13.250
an incredible variety of
software as a service capabilities.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:19.410
So this is actually quite an old slide, it's
six years old.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:26.360
Bessemer, venture capital
firm, hasn't created a new version of it recently,

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:28.040
at least not in this format.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:35.991
But this was a their attempt to capture
as I said the top 300 companies that were

00:11:35.991 --> 00:11:39.740
providing various forms of software as a service
or platform as a

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:43.510
service capabilities to enterprises.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:50.820
Of course, a few of these are relevant to
science, most of them are not.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:57.060
Hopefully, I believe we're going to see an
emergence of a more scientifically focused

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:03.220
software and platform as a service offerings
in the coming years.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:09.020
I
mentioned in the abstract for this lecture

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:11.350
and I'll mention here.

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:17.830
Well, myself and my colleague, Dennis Gannon,
recently produced a book called Cloud Computing

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:21.450
for Science
and Engineering that really goes far more

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:29.700
deeply than I can in this hour or so-- into
this question of what cloud

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.400
computing is and how it can be used for science
and engineering.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.860
So I'm going to use a variety of components
from that book as I proceed through the rest

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.220
of this lecture.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:46.980
And I should emphasize that this is not just
a bad sales job.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:51.050
You can buy this book, but the contents are
all entirely

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:54.470
online at cloud4scieng.org.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:13:00.050
So you're able to enjoy it without some purchasing
the book.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:05.430
Although, it does make a
nice present for your family and so forth.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:12.550
Anyway, so let me just show you what goes
into the online system because it has a couple

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:13.550
of interesting features.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:20.740
So, of course, we brought the contents of
the book and available in something that looks

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:21.880
quite pleasant.

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:24.360
And you'll
see sort of what the topics we cover, and

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:28.279
these are the topics and then a review very
briefly in this lecture.

00:13:28.279 --> 00:13:34.870
How you use the cloud as a platform-- sorry
as a virtual computer.

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:36.440
How you manage data in the cloud.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:38.640
How you
can compute in the cloud.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.950
And then how you use the cloud as a platform.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:45.930
How you make the services that are
available for things like data analytics,

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.980
streaming, machine learning.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:53.440
I'll say a bit about something called the
Globus research data management platform,

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:56.570
which is cloud hosted and is often used in
science.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:14:02.050
I won't talk today, but the book does talk
a bit about how to build your own cloud.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:10.430
How to configure a cluster of
your own to run some of these data management

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:12.760
and computing management services.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:16.269
And I won't also talk
today about security and privacy, but there's

00:14:16.269 --> 00:14:19.841
a lot of material in the book on that.

00:14:19.841 --> 00:14:25.120
And of course, that's a very
important topic for many purposes.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:27.240
So that's the content of the book.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:32.200
The other interesting thing here, and I'd
really like to just convey it to you briefly

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:36.790
is we have a set of Jupiter notebooks.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:44.360
Hopefully, many of you are familiar with Jupiter,
a web-based interactive

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:45.720
computing environment.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:56.310
So we have some 23, I think it is, Jupiter
notebooks that allow you to try-- well, observe

00:14:56.310 --> 00:15:03.860
or experiment with develop applications using
the various technologies that are described

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:04.860
in this book.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:09.260
And this is what a Jupiter notebook looks
like for those of you who are not familiar

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.260
with it.

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:12.420
And here we are using it
to run the Python.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:16.850
We use Python really for all of the examples
in the book pretty much.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:22.610
And Jupiter notebooks
has a series of cells, each of which contain

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:30.180
Python code, and you can run the cells online
using your web browser.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:39.029
And you create inline graphics or, as we often
do, manipulate and access cloud computing

00:15:39.029 --> 00:15:43.339
services to do things
like retrieve data, store data, or run computing

00:15:43.339 --> 00:15:44.830
and then so forth.

00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:53.570
OK, so let me just spend a few minutes now
talking about some of the capabilities that

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:59.920
you're going to get from cloud computing at
the infrastructure level.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:02.460
The
cloud is virtual computer.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:06.730
So what does your desktop computer provide
to you?

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:09.080
It provides computing and storage.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:12.529
It does other things
because, of course of, the software that it

00:16:12.529 --> 00:16:13.529
runs.

00:16:13.529 --> 00:16:16.260
But computing and storage are the two fundamental
capabilities.

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:27.320
And similarly, cloud computing providers offer
a very rich set of storage as a service options.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:31.970
And these implement
as we show here a variety of different storage

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:32.970
models.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:39.839
There are certainly file system models so
that you can read and write files just as

00:16:39.839 --> 00:16:44.029
you would on your desktop
machine.

00:16:44.029 --> 00:16:49.899
But there are also other important storage
models offered as well.

00:16:49.899 --> 00:16:57.269
So object storage was the first cloud
storage service provided by Amazon some quite

00:16:57.269 --> 00:17:05.579
a few years ago now and is one of the most
popular.

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:15.859
It's important relates to the fact that it's
highly scalable, reliable and relatively inexpensive

00:17:15.859 --> 00:17:18.519
compared to some of
the others.

00:17:18.519 --> 00:17:22.569
But as well as objects storage, which we'll
say more about in a minute.

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:27.329
They provide relational
database citizens, NoSQL database services,

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:32.750
graph database services, warehouse analytics
services and so

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:33.750
forth.

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:39.749
What I'm showing here in terms of each of
these columns is what offerings each of the

00:17:39.749 --> 00:17:43.929
three major cloud vendors
provides-- Amazon, Google, and Azure.

00:17:43.929 --> 00:17:49.490
And you'll see that they all have often very
similar, sometimes rather

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:52.679
different versions of these capabilities.

00:17:52.679 --> 00:18:00.149
So it's not unusual now to see people building
data analytics services that

00:18:00.149 --> 00:18:09.659
maintain the data that's being manipulated
in an object store or relational data service.

00:18:09.659 --> 00:18:16.509
Compute on the data on the cloud and then
write results back to other cloud services

00:18:16.509 --> 00:18:18.720
before [INAUDIBLE] the
computing.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.649
And we'll see examples of that in a second.

00:18:21.649 --> 00:18:26.480
A few words about the object storage model.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:31.859
The basic
idea is similar across all systems.

00:18:31.859 --> 00:18:42.440
Your storage universe is divided up into buckets
called-- sometimes, they're

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:43.690
called containers.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:48.320
They're sometimes called buckets.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:54.440
And within a bucket, you can place often very
large numbers of objects.

00:18:54.440 --> 00:19:03.789
And the the cloud service then provides
the application programming interfaces and

00:19:03.789 --> 00:19:08.240
other methods for putting things into containers,
for retrieving them

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:16.320
from containers, or associating attributes
with containers and so forth.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:22.580
So we'll see an example of that in a minute.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:29.309
But one other thing we're showing here is
that one thing that the object storage commonly

00:19:29.309 --> 00:19:33.331
support is in some form
of versioning, which can be important, of

00:19:33.331 --> 00:19:38.419
course, if you are concerned about reproducible
science and being able

00:19:38.419 --> 00:19:42.669
to track changes to your data over time.

00:19:42.669 --> 00:19:44.619
So that's the object model.

00:19:44.619 --> 00:19:55.820
I'll just show briefly how it is realized
in one particular cloud service, Amazon.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.679
And I use
this example really to convey an other notion

00:19:59.679 --> 00:20:06.100
which is that most of the services that cloud
providers offer support

00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:09.799
both web interfaces for interactive use.

00:20:09.799 --> 00:20:15.490
So the top here, I'm creating a new buck called
Cloud for Science.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:20.280
You'll see what I get [INAUDIBLE] where I
want to create it.

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:22.940
No, I can't place it.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:27.879
There are different data centers
operated by cloud providers and depending

00:20:27.879 --> 00:20:30.820
on where I think most of the accesses will
come.

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:33.739
[INAUDIBLE] may
place it one location or another.

00:20:33.739 --> 00:20:38.729
And, of course, being able to use a web interface
is often a very nice way of

00:20:38.729 --> 00:20:40.419
manipulating things.

00:20:40.419 --> 00:20:47.769
But then typically there are application programming
interfaces in software development kits

00:20:47.769 --> 00:20:54.429
that allow me to do anything I can do with
a web interface and often more programmatically.

00:20:54.429 --> 00:21:01.019
So down in the bottom, I've got some Python
code that's basically doing the same thing

00:21:01.019 --> 00:21:02.019
as I just showed here.

00:21:02.019 --> 00:21:08.919
First of all, I delete the bucket I just created
with the web interface.

00:21:08.919 --> 00:21:11.799
And then I create it specifying that I want
it

00:21:11.799 --> 00:21:14.679
located in the US standard region.

00:21:14.679 --> 00:21:20.309
And then I put in put an object into that
nearly created bucket.

00:21:20.309 --> 00:21:28.309
So you can do some amazingly sophisticated
things using fairly simple Python code.

00:21:28.309 --> 00:21:32.440
So I won't to say more about
storage.

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:35.359
There are some other wonderful storage services.

00:21:35.359 --> 00:21:39.809
Let me say a bit about computing in the cloud
now.

00:21:39.809 --> 00:21:44.899
So
sort of just summarize some of the key concepts

00:21:44.899 --> 00:21:50.190
by showing the table of contents from the
online book.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:22:00.239
So computing as a service at the most fundamental
level is offered typically in the form of

00:22:00.239 --> 00:22:02.269
either visual machines
or containers.

00:22:02.269 --> 00:22:08.440
And I'll say more about is between those in
a minute.

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:13.470
Serverless computing, I'll also say more about
a little bit further on.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:23.381
But each cloud service offers mechanisms that'll
allow you to create virtual machines, create

00:22:23.381 --> 00:22:28.600
one instance of a virtual machine-- 10, 100,
1,000-- configured with whatever software

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.399
you want.

00:22:30.399 --> 00:22:41.580
And then there are services that let you manage
large numbers of such virtual machines.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:46.169
For example, you may
run MapReduce computations across them.

00:22:46.169 --> 00:22:52.220
You might define a computing graph or workflow
that runs on them.

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:58.369
You can also run message passing interface
computations across them.

00:22:58.369 --> 00:23:09.429
The cloud systems don't tend to offer the
ultra high speed communication interfaces

00:23:09.429 --> 00:23:14.019
that might be required for
high performance computing, [INAUDIBLE] at

00:23:14.019 --> 00:23:15.159
very large scale.

00:23:15.159 --> 00:23:18.159
But they seem to do reasonably well up to
at least

00:23:18.159 --> 00:23:27.360
a few hundred nodes in many cases, which is
often what one needs for ones or scientific

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:28.360
applications.

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:30.519
Unless
you're dealing with extremely large scale

00:23:30.519 --> 00:23:31.970
simulations.

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:37.519
So those are some of the concepts, I think--
serverless is sort of special and we'll come

00:23:37.519 --> 00:23:38.519
back to that.

00:23:38.519 --> 00:23:40.759
But the rest
of them are the sorts of things that you might

00:23:40.759 --> 00:23:45.630
reasonably do on your local computing system.

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:52.989
A little bit about so
virtual machines versus containers.

00:23:52.989 --> 00:23:59.019
Perhaps many of you are familiar with the
distinctions here.

00:23:59.019 --> 00:24:05.509
A container is, in some sense, a more lightweight
version of a virtual.

00:24:05.509 --> 00:24:14.149
A virtual machine is something that lets you
run a particular application in a particular

00:24:14.149 --> 00:24:18.659
set of libraries in a particular operating
system on a computer without

00:24:18.659 --> 00:24:24.539
having to have that computer dedicated to
that operating system.

00:24:24.539 --> 00:24:29.490
So if you're a software is designed to run
on a

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:37.059
particular version of Linux or a particular
version of Windows, you can stand up a virtual

00:24:37.059 --> 00:24:42.299
machine with that
software, including the operating system,

00:24:42.299 --> 00:24:44.210
deployed and ready to go.

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:51.690
And containers work in a similar fashion except
they organize themselves slightly differently

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:54.370
internally.

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:59.009
And as the
table with the bottom points out, containers

00:24:59.009 --> 00:25:05.570
are being increasingly used for many things,
including data science

00:25:05.570 --> 00:25:07.600
because they're more lightweight.

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:09.919
They can be deployed very rapidly.

00:25:09.919 --> 00:25:13.750
They can be created and destroyed rapidly.

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:21.429
And for various reasons they are quite portable
from one cloud to another thanks to technologies

00:25:21.429 --> 00:25:27.559
like Docker and
Singularity which I won't say more about.

00:25:27.559 --> 00:25:35.429
So just point out that as with storage, so
with computing, the cloud

00:25:35.429 --> 00:25:41.759
providers offer web interfaces that let start
and managed instances.

00:25:41.759 --> 00:25:52.080
So here I'm looking at a Amazon compute
virtual machine interface.

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:59.720
And you can see I've got three actual machine
instances-- two are stopped; one is running.

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:02.119
In fact, it's in the
middle of the initializing.

00:26:02.119 --> 00:26:04.440
And I could launch additional ones if I wanted.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:08.909
So just with a click of a button, I can create
a new computing system.

00:26:08.909 --> 00:26:13.049
And, of course, if I don't want to click buttons,
in Azure Storage I can also create

00:26:13.049 --> 00:26:17.639
instances using, for example, a Python API.

00:26:17.639 --> 00:26:25.220
Or so on the bottom here, I'm creating one
instance of a particular type, a micro type,

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:31.220
so [INAUDIBLE] not a very
powerful instance using a specified virtual

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:34.539
machine image in the US west region.

00:26:34.539 --> 00:26:43.489
I could also be creating 1,000
instances of very powerful computers using

00:26:43.489 --> 00:26:44.609
the same API.

00:26:44.609 --> 00:26:50.489
To give you a feeling for what sorts of things
that you

00:26:50.489 --> 00:26:51.909
can do with these capabilities.

00:26:51.909 --> 00:27:06.009
So this is a pipeline developed by a group
at Chicago working with people at USC and

00:27:06.009 --> 00:27:12.389
ISD and in Seattle.

00:27:12.389 --> 00:27:16.059
So what
they wanted to do was-- I won't go to the

00:27:16.059 --> 00:27:22.479
technical details-- but to run a computational
process that would find

00:27:22.479 --> 00:27:32.629
potential [INAUDIBLE] binding sites within
large amounts of DNA's [? secA ?] data from

00:27:32.629 --> 00:27:33.960
encode.

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:46.849
And the pipeline involved running various
popular applications-- SNAP Aligner, [INAUDIBLE],

00:27:46.849 --> 00:27:53.869
Tool, a couple of
different footprint finding applications.

00:27:53.869 --> 00:28:02.460
And running this over large amounts, terabytes
of data-- well, actually it's

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:11.119
more tens of gigabytes of data but producing
along the way terabytes of data from as a

00:28:11.119 --> 00:28:12.549
side effect of the
computation.

00:28:12.549 --> 00:28:19.871
So this is something that would be pretty
hard to run on one's desktop or even in a

00:28:19.871 --> 00:28:21.469
typical lab.

00:28:21.469 --> 00:28:24.879
But because we
use Amazon Web Services, we can fire this

00:28:24.879 --> 00:28:30.340
up on many multi-core nodes and run the whole
thing in a couple of

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:37.090
hours I think using, in this case, 749 load
hours during computation.

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:45.690
And then the results of the data is retrieved
from the encode system.

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:51.200
The results are eventually deployed onto
Amazon storage.

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:58.279
So it isn't a typical many scientific applications,
we are using a mix of cloud and non-cloud

00:28:58.279 --> 00:29:01.359
computing resources to do what we want to
do.

00:29:01.359 --> 00:29:10.369
I just mentioned very briefly that for those
in the scientific space

00:29:10.369 --> 00:29:17.899
who are interested in working with cloud computing
but don't want to use public cloud resources

00:29:17.899 --> 00:29:24.330
[AUDIO OUT]
There are a number of academic cloud providers

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:26.499
that one can work with.

00:29:26.499 --> 00:29:30.690
Perhaps, the most accessible is
Jetstream.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:36.580
It's operated by the National Science Foundation,
or operated for the National Science Foundation

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:40.190
by
the University of Indiana.

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:49.679
So jetstreamcloud.org, you can head there
and request a start up account quite easily.

00:29:49.679 --> 00:29:56.070
And then you can do the sorts of things I've
been showing already-- create a virtual machine,

00:29:56.070 --> 00:30:00.989
create containers,
store data, and so on and so forth.

00:30:00.989 --> 00:30:05.779
My [INAUDIBLE] in Chicago uses this quite
a bit and they find it very, very easy

00:30:05.779 --> 00:30:08.489
to do work [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:08.489 --> 00:30:15.409
OK, a few words about the cloud as a virtual
computer, a place where you can store data

00:30:15.409 --> 00:30:16.609
and compute.

00:30:16.609 --> 00:30:27.009
But what I think makes the cloud really exciting
is its capabilities in terms of our platform.

00:30:27.009 --> 00:30:28.940
So what do I mean by a
platform here?

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:36.679
It's set of services that cloud providers
operate that you can access via APIs and,

00:30:36.679 --> 00:30:41.090
therefore,
integrate into your applications, which may

00:30:41.090 --> 00:30:46.619
run either locally or remotely-- either on
the cloud or on your own

00:30:46.619 --> 00:30:52.940
computer-- to do things that would be very
difficult for you to do locally.

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:58.070
So I guess the storage services we've already
talked about already have a sort of a platform

00:30:58.070 --> 00:30:59.220
characteristic.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:06.320
If I
want to write an application that runs in

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:14.149
my lab and stores all the data coming off
my estimates into a object store

00:31:14.149 --> 00:31:20.889
while indexing them in a database, I can do
that via a simple point on a script that calls

00:31:20.889 --> 00:31:26.259
suitable cloud storage
APIs.

00:31:26.259 --> 00:31:32.039
But the services that we describe in this
slide and the next one actually allow us to

00:31:32.039 --> 00:31:35.539
do a lot more than simply
store a bit of data.

00:31:35.539 --> 00:31:38.659
So let me say just a few words about each
of them.

00:31:38.659 --> 00:31:45.229
So there's a variety of data analytics services.

00:31:45.229 --> 00:31:49.139
Some of these
are simply things that let you run method

00:31:49.139 --> 00:31:51.129
use computations or spark.

00:31:51.129 --> 00:31:53.979
They're actually spark computations.

00:31:53.979 --> 00:31:59.499
So
computations that perform parallel analysis

00:31:59.499 --> 00:32:02.470
of a large quantities of data.

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:08.909
There are other services like Google Cloud
Datalab lab and Amazon Athena Analytics that

00:32:08.909 --> 00:32:13.220
provide more
sophisticated built in analytic capabilities.

00:32:13.220 --> 00:32:20.599
There's a very rich set of services offered
by various providers that deal

00:32:20.599 --> 00:32:23.169
with data streaming.

00:32:23.169 --> 00:32:31.409
So data coming off a scientific instrument,
you might might set up a streaming service

00:32:31.409 --> 00:32:37.559
that
would receive the each image as it is produced

00:32:37.559 --> 00:32:39.529
and then does something with it.

00:32:39.529 --> 00:32:46.629
Perhaps, processes it, stores the results
in a database, notifies people that it's available.

00:32:46.629 --> 00:32:49.889
Those are the sorts of
things that one that implement very easily

00:32:49.889 --> 00:32:56.809
using things like Google Dataflow, Kafka,
and Kinesis from Amazon and

00:32:56.809 --> 00:32:58.399
other [INAUDIBLE] services.

00:32:58.399 --> 00:33:02.740
I may say a bit more about that later if time
permits.

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:11.619
And then there's a growing set of very powerful
machine learning services being provided by

00:33:11.619 --> 00:33:17.259
a all of the major
cloud providers.

00:33:17.259 --> 00:33:24.719
And I think this is a particularly exciting
set of capabilities for various reasons.

00:33:24.719 --> 00:33:30.940
In particular
because good machine learning often requires

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:34.630
access to powerful computing capabilities.

00:33:34.630 --> 00:33:39.150
Specialized capabilities
like GPUs, graphical processing units, large

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:42.609
amounts of data, large quantities of computing.

00:33:42.609 --> 00:33:53.509
And these are all our easily accessible for
a price needless to say on these commercial

00:33:53.509 --> 00:33:55.630
cloud providers.

00:33:55.630 --> 00:34:01.869
And they
also provide those computing resources along

00:34:01.869 --> 00:34:11.020
with pre-configured software to that implements
of various machine

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:12.640
learning methods.

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:20.390
And partly that's possible because there is
such a rich collection of open source software

00:34:20.390 --> 00:34:24.840
for
various forms of machine learning, deep learning

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:27.620
and other similar techniques.

00:34:27.620 --> 00:34:33.230
Partly because these cloud vendors see this
as a differentiator.

00:34:33.230 --> 00:34:35.820
So they're constantly competing to try and
offer

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:40.640
the best set of this such software on their
platforms.

00:34:40.640 --> 00:34:45.960
So down at the bottom, I just described briefly
one of the

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:47.929
services that Amazon offers.

00:34:47.929 --> 00:34:55.120
It has a pre-configured virtual machine image
which implements a variety of

00:34:55.120 --> 00:35:04.610
different-- AMI, by the way, is Amazon Machine
Image.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:08.710
So they have a deep learning AMI which includes
a whole range of different platforms.

00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:11.851
I have lots of examples and
so forth.

00:35:11.851 --> 00:35:17.550
So we can fairly quickly run through and apply
some of these methods.

00:35:17.550 --> 00:35:26.870
So one of the notebooks that we
supply, number 21, we use this to see what

00:35:26.870 --> 00:35:31.540
happens when we try and apply a pre-trained
model.

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:37.200
A model that's
been trained on a large publicly available

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:38.300
image net-- the data set.

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:42.100
The data set is called image net-- to some
biological images.

00:35:42.100 --> 00:35:44.970
So I won't go through all of the code.

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:48.630
It's only 50 lines or so of code that set
this up.

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:51.510
It loads in the image net data.

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:54.600
It loads in the image net model.

00:35:54.600 --> 00:36:03.830
And then we apply this to our four images
that we featured in the resulting model.

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:14.680
And so we've got some yeast, the bacteria,
an amoeba, and a seahorse.

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:18.830
And then we're taking the pre-train model.

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:20.550
It's been trained on public data.

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:24.960
It isn't particularly specialized for
biology, but nevertheless, you'll see they

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:33.850
come up with a set of labels for these images
along with their-- like some

00:36:33.850 --> 00:36:36.200
measure of their confidence.

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:43.260
And then you see the top ratings for each
of them is actually correct.

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:45.290
So they all do
well.

00:36:45.290 --> 00:36:50.330
The yeast and seahorse, their confidence is
less high.

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:58.930
But nevertheless, it does show you that with
essentially no

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:05.290
effort, it's possible to apply some quite
sophisticated machine learning techniques

00:37:05.290 --> 00:37:06.290
to data.

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:10.900
I mean, I could be
running this labeling system and data coming

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:16.300
out of a lab and sometimes it might provide
some useful capabilities.

00:37:16.300 --> 00:37:20.440
Of course, if we were doing this seriously,
it would take forever.

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:26.300
Versions of this network that have been trained
perhaps with more larger quantities of more

00:37:26.300 --> 00:37:28.060
biologically relevant data.

00:37:28.060 --> 00:37:34.100
And nevertheless, we can do very well just
out of the gate.

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:41.270
Now, let me mention something else that think
is important about cloud computing.

00:37:41.270 --> 00:37:49.560
And this is how one may develop applications
for cloud platforms.

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:54.850
So I talked briefly virtual machine, showed
how

00:37:54.850 --> 00:38:03.970
you stand up with virtual machine, which virtual
machine can you contain just the software

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:07.230
that you would normally
run on your workstation.

00:38:07.230 --> 00:38:15.340
It might run an application that simply reads
in some data or apply some data analytics

00:38:15.340 --> 00:38:18.140
function to it and writes out the results.

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:22.360
And that is basically doing what we do at
home but doing it in the cloud

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:23.500
instead of locally.

00:38:23.500 --> 00:38:29.320
We might be doing that for reasons of stability
or access to specialized resources.

00:38:29.320 --> 00:38:35.001
But increasingly people are
developing applications on the cloud in a

00:38:35.001 --> 00:38:36.340
rather different manner.

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:38.280
And I think it's important to understand that
if

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:42.640
you start thinking about using cloud for your
own purposes.

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:45.640
And what they're doing is building what people
call

00:38:45.640 --> 00:38:48.790
cloud native applications.

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:53.860
And this picture down here really shows what's
involved.

00:38:53.860 --> 00:38:59.300
So on the left, we have how we might build
an application

00:38:59.300 --> 00:39:05.900
on a desktop perhaps or on the cloud.

00:39:05.900 --> 00:39:11.800
You have a set of virtual machines or containers,
each of which has some

00:39:11.800 --> 00:39:15.360
data in it and some computing logic.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:22.360
And they do whatever our data analytics or
other computations that we want

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:23.590
to perform.

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:28.580
So these are systems of what we call stateful.

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:32.040
Each of these virtual machines has its own
local file system in which

00:39:32.040 --> 00:39:36.960
it's storing data or if it wants to communicate
data to another virtual machine, perhaps it

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:42.300
writes it to a shared file
system or sends it a message or something.

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:46.070
But the reliability of these sorts of applications
is potentially

00:39:46.070 --> 00:39:54.960
challenging because if your virtual machine
fails, then you've lost the data that was

00:39:54.960 --> 00:39:55.960
stored in it.

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:57.120
So that's not ideal.

00:39:57.120 --> 00:40:05.800
A cloud native application will typically
be structured in this sort of format, "all

00:40:05.800 --> 00:40:06.800
state."

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:11.380
The data you are analyzing,
the results of an analysis and so forth are

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:18.350
stored in some cloud storage service.

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:23.270
And these are designed to be
highly reliable or replicate data across multiple

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:27.810
geographic regions so they'll maintain multiple
copies of the data.

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:33.380
And they'll keep track of which copies are
up to date and so forth.

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:39.130
And then the computation is performed by a
stateless computation, stateless services,

00:40:39.130 --> 00:40:46.940
that might to get created
just for the purpose of performing your analysis.

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:49.120
If they fail-- no problem.

00:40:49.120 --> 00:40:50.610
You simply rerun the analysis.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:56.240
And this
has a number of advantages, it's the resulting

00:40:56.240 --> 00:41:01.970
systems are more reliable.

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:07.710
They can also be more easily scaled up
and down as the amount of computation that

00:41:07.710 --> 00:41:10.370
needs to be formed that changes over time.

00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:15.900
So anyway, this is a
quite an old example that I still think it's

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:16.900
a very good one.

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:20.650
This is the structures used by an application
called ANIMOTO.

00:41:20.650 --> 00:41:25.460
I don't know if any of you have heard of it.

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:28.520
It's a
system that takes a set of slides-- oh, no

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:34.620
sorry pictures-- and creates a little music
video with the pictures that you

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:38.270
provide to it, so no scientific interest.

00:41:38.270 --> 00:41:45.040
But since it's not atypical of many data analysis
applications, in science

00:41:45.040 --> 00:41:47.170
you've got some input images.

00:41:47.170 --> 00:41:52.180
You do some processing of them and then some
further processing.

00:41:52.180 --> 00:41:55.890
After you've done the analysis, you do, in
this

00:41:55.890 --> 00:41:56.890
case, rendering.

00:41:56.890 --> 00:42:04.790
And then you distribute the results, perhaps
it could be pushing the data to a public repository

00:42:04.790 --> 00:42:07.570
or
making it available to people in some other

00:42:07.570 --> 00:42:08.570
way.

00:42:08.570 --> 00:42:14.270
And this is implemented as a cloud native
application.

00:42:14.270 --> 00:42:21.680
So all states are stored in an object store,
the Amazon S3 object store.

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:25.770
And there's a set of virtual machines
varying numbers of them over time that handle

00:42:25.770 --> 00:42:33.000
things like interact upload, data upload,
the analysis, the

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:34.860
rendering, and distribution.

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:41.080
If nothing is happening, maybe no virtual
machines are associated or perhaps just one

00:42:41.080 --> 00:42:42.080
of each.

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:46.880
As load increases then more virtual machines
are provided.

00:42:46.880 --> 00:42:51.360
You'll see a reference here to a service called
Amazon SQS.

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:57.040
This is the Simple Queuing Service which is
one of a number of similar services operated

00:42:57.040 --> 00:43:02.210
by
different cloud providers that basically does

00:43:02.210 --> 00:43:06.830
what you would think it-- it operates a queue
into which your request

00:43:06.830 --> 00:43:16.960
to do things can be placed in from which--
so your virtual machine can extract our elements.

00:43:16.960 --> 00:43:25.420
So cloud native is, as I said, increasingly
the way that people build applications.

00:43:25.420 --> 00:43:28.840
So if you simply want to analyze
some data of your own, you're not going to

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:31.720
be particularly concerned about cloud native.

00:43:31.720 --> 00:43:36.760
But if you want to create
a service that will analyze lots of data for

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:43.400
lots of people then you might well want to
structure it in the form that I've

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:45.330
just shown you.

00:43:45.330 --> 00:43:51.320
And this is increasingly easy to do given
the capabilities that are offered by the various

00:43:51.320 --> 00:43:53.670
cloud
providers.

00:43:53.670 --> 00:44:00.350
One interesting step beyond cloud native is
what people call a Function-as-a-Service.

00:44:00.350 --> 00:44:07.280
So in the example I just
showed you, we have these virtual machines--

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:09.360
you create the virtual machine.

00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:16.220
It runs some basic logic-- you know
wait for a task to be available, do some computation,

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:20.070
store the result back into storage.

00:44:20.070 --> 00:44:26.190
Sorry, wrong way.

00:44:26.190 --> 00:44:32.620
In what's called Function-as-a-Service, the
whole need to create and manage these virtual

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:35.300
machines is automated.

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:41.940
So that all one has to do is simply specify
a function that you want to have executed

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:45.210
when
certain events occur.

00:44:45.210 --> 00:44:51.760
And so, for example, here is a simple example.

00:44:51.760 --> 00:45:00.150
This is a function that responds to events--
messages appearing on this system I mentioned

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:03.180
briefly earlier, Kinesis.

00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:09.110
So the event might be that a new image is
available from an instrument in your lab.

00:45:09.110 --> 00:45:14.670
We're using it-- at least, it's for
this purpose that I want.

00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:21.820
And each time such an event happens, we've
configured this function so that it will be

00:45:21.820 --> 00:45:30.260
executed being past the details of the event
and some context and-- You don't need to read

00:45:30.260 --> 00:45:37.650
the details, but what
you'll see it does is it reaches inside the

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:43.530
event, grab some information, and then loads
it into a database, a

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:45.890
dynamodb, db-- database.

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:55.510
So 15 lines or so of code, I've specified
what needs to happen whenever the data is

00:45:55.510 --> 00:45:57.420
generated by an instrument in
my lab.

00:45:57.420 --> 00:46:01.900
And I don't need to be worrying about managing
virtual machines or scaling up the number

00:46:01.900 --> 00:46:06.940
of machines
and circle I just need to specify the function.

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:12.900
OK, getting to sort of one more topic I want
to cover, and then we'll

00:46:12.900 --> 00:46:13.900
wrap up.

00:46:13.900 --> 00:46:26.400
So one of the things that we as scientists
can do with cloud is to use it to provide

00:46:26.400 --> 00:46:30.810
services to other scientists.

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:34.780
And
my group at Chicago does this, we operate

00:46:34.780 --> 00:46:40.600
a service called Globus which provides some
capabilities for research

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:41.600
data management.

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:47.820
And specifically, I show here two of them,
the ability to manage the transfer of data

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:52.790
from, for
example, a sequencing center to cloud storage

00:46:52.790 --> 00:46:55.810
or to your institutional storage.

00:46:55.810 --> 00:47:01.490
And then manage who's allowed to access sharing
of that data.

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:05.560
And this global software is actually a cloud
made

00:47:05.560 --> 00:47:08.090
of servers, it runs on Amazon.

00:47:08.090 --> 00:47:15.050
So every time you make a request to it, it
is a message gets queued in similar

00:47:15.050 --> 00:47:17.850
fashion to what I showed a few minutes ago.

00:47:17.850 --> 00:47:27.780
It's dequeued, a transfer is negotiated and
managed and tracked as

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:29.240
it receives.

00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:37.460
If you request that certain data be shared
with other people and similar various tables

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:42.150
will be updated to reflect the
fact that some other users of is allowed to

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:43.620
access your data.

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:47.250
The data itself doesn't need to go near the
cloud, it's

00:47:47.250 --> 00:47:53.500
just the control logic that runs very reliably
and securely on Amazon because that's what

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:56.740
cloud platforms are good
for.

00:47:56.740 --> 00:48:04.140
And like any other cloud service, there's
a web interface which looks a bit like this.

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:10.790
There's also a Python API
so you can say that I want to move data from

00:48:10.790 --> 00:48:16.410
one computer to another by using this submit_transfer
request and

00:48:16.410 --> 00:48:21.380
then waiting for it to complete it if that
is what I want to do.

00:48:21.380 --> 00:48:29.390
OK, so that is a very brief review of cloud
computing and why it matters for data science.

00:48:29.390 --> 00:48:37.910
So just to remind you,
the cloud is-- you can view it as a virtual

00:48:37.910 --> 00:48:42.110
computer where you can manage data and perform
computing.

00:48:42.110 --> 00:48:45.650
You can
also view it as a platform that you can link

00:48:45.650 --> 00:48:48.660
into your own applications that you can use
to build new sorts of

00:48:48.660 --> 00:48:56.080
applications that perform things like data
analytics, management of streaming data, and

00:48:56.080 --> 00:49:01.730
machine learning, data
movement and so forth without having to deploy

00:49:01.730 --> 00:49:07.460
a lot of complex software on your own local
computers.

00:49:07.460 --> 00:49:11.510
So why, therefore, does the cloud matter for
data science?

00:49:11.510 --> 00:49:14.800
Well, these are reasons that occurred to me.

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:17.900
You may
have others.

00:49:17.900 --> 00:49:21.980
It certainly provides you with on-demand computing
and storage.

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:24.460
Of course, it comes at a price.

00:49:24.460 --> 00:49:26.670
You
will have to pay for these services, but for

00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:33.300
many computing applications the costs are
modest relative to the time

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:37.390
that you might spend meeting those costs in
other ways.

00:49:37.390 --> 00:49:41.260
You can get access to specialized hardware
certainly, graphical processing units and

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:43.430
so forth.

00:49:43.430 --> 00:49:46.090
What I find most
exciting, of course, is this rich collection

00:49:46.090 --> 00:49:50.820
of platform services that let you build new
classes of applications.

00:49:50.820 --> 00:49:53.230
For
example, using this cloud native programming

00:49:53.230 --> 00:49:54.470
model.

00:49:54.470 --> 00:49:57.000
And you can do these things in a way that
achieves

00:49:57.000 --> 00:49:59.430
sustainability-- high availability.

00:49:59.430 --> 00:50:07.290
But you will read occasionally about events
when the cloud services become inaccessible,

00:50:07.290 --> 00:50:11.120
but they're only news
because they're so rare.

00:50:11.120 --> 00:50:18.000
They're certainly more available than any
computing platform that I have access at any

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:22.190
institution or lab that I might work with.

00:50:22.190 --> 00:50:28.890
And similarily, the security-- you've got
very complex and sophisticated

00:50:28.890 --> 00:50:33.010
systems in place to manage the security of
these platforms.

00:50:33.010 --> 00:50:38.630
Increasingly, people are using them infact
to manage

00:50:38.630 --> 00:50:42.440
data and so forth and finding that works well
for them.

00:50:42.440 --> 00:50:46.130
So I think that's when the last slide, so
thank you very much

00:50:46.130 --> 00:50:47.400
for your attention.

00:50:47.400 --> 00:50:49.090
Thank you so much, Ian.

00:50:49.090 --> 00:50:54.790
That was a great overview of cloud computing
and why it should matter for data science.

00:50:54.790 --> 00:51:01.790
I would like to ask folks who are listening
in, so if you do have any questions for Dr.

00:51:01.790 --> 00:51:05.390
Foster to please submit them
via the question submission system.

00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:07.520
And I'll do my best to get to them.

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:11.450
One question that we had, you mentioned
it sort of at the end there-- I think it's

00:51:11.450 --> 00:51:17.800
probably worth some further thoughts from
you-- is the notion of security and

00:51:17.800 --> 00:51:24.860
whether-- just at a time kind of when people
are getting used to the notion of cloud computing,

00:51:24.860 --> 00:51:29.300
we're starting to
see some sort of bad examples of potential

00:51:29.300 --> 00:51:30.300
security issues.

00:51:30.300 --> 00:51:33.150
I know there was a issue with a couple of
things that

00:51:33.150 --> 00:51:38.590
go by the terms of Meltdown and Spectre which
sounds terrible.

00:51:38.590 --> 00:51:41.150
What sort of implications did these types
of things

00:51:41.150 --> 00:51:47.630
and other security issues have for cloud computing
and peoples' and institutions', I guess, comfort

00:51:47.630 --> 00:51:49.330
with it.

00:51:49.330 --> 00:51:53.610
Well, those are good questions.

00:51:53.610 --> 00:52:02.360
My view is in general that a cloud computing
platform is going to be more secure

00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:14.200
than any institution's computing platform
simply because there are more people, more

00:52:14.200 --> 00:52:18.590
highly trained people, more
sophisticated people managing security in

00:52:18.590 --> 00:52:23.360
a place like Amazon.

00:52:23.360 --> 00:52:30.070
There are these recent events that you
mentioned, they're certainly of concern.

00:52:30.070 --> 00:52:33.890
I think they're a concern for anyone using--
whoops.

00:52:33.890 --> 00:52:40.250
I don't know what
happened there-- anyone who is using computers

00:52:40.250 --> 00:52:45.160
anywhere, I'm sure the cloud vendors are on
top of them.

00:52:45.160 --> 00:52:55.770
There are versions-- I should also mention
there are different levels of security in

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:58.810
the cloud.

00:52:58.810 --> 00:53:03.290
Some of the things
that one has to worry about in your local

00:53:03.290 --> 00:53:05.600
computers, you also have to worry about there.

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:10.150
You have to make sure
that you're running the latest version of

00:53:10.150 --> 00:53:11.150
software.

00:53:11.150 --> 00:53:13.710
That's important.

00:53:13.710 --> 00:53:24.970
There are different levels of-- for example
Amazon runs the regular cloud and also dove

00:53:24.970 --> 00:53:32.680
cloud, which is designed
for highly secure data that, for example,

00:53:32.680 --> 00:53:33.690
can't leave the country.

00:53:33.690 --> 00:53:40.250
And so you also need to be aware of what the
capabilities are that they might make available

00:53:40.250 --> 00:53:42.270
to you.

00:53:42.270 --> 00:53:43.270
Of course.

00:53:43.270 --> 00:53:49.050
So you're based in part at Argonne National
Laboratory, which I presume is involved in

00:53:49.050 --> 00:53:54.190
a lot of atom
smashing and other sorts of particle physics

00:53:54.190 --> 00:53:55.310
and other types of things.

00:53:55.310 --> 00:54:00.870
And I imagine that you've got a lot of
experience in examples of physics and astronomy

00:54:00.870 --> 00:54:06.300
and possibly working with CERN and other major
organizations

00:54:06.300 --> 00:54:10.740
looking at either the very small or the very
distant.

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:13.290
Maybe you could say a couple of comments on
that and what's

00:54:13.290 --> 00:54:17.090
kind of come out of some of your interactions
with those folks, and also if you've been

00:54:17.090 --> 00:54:21.620
doing any stuff that has
biomedical relevance that people might be

00:54:21.620 --> 00:54:25.860
interested in hearing about.

00:54:25.860 --> 00:54:38.250
Well, so, looking particularly at Argonne,
one area that we spend a fair bit of time

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:43.730
on is working with specialized
experimental facilities, like the Advance

00:54:43.730 --> 00:54:47.600
Photon Source which high energy X-ray source.

00:54:47.600 --> 00:54:52.540
It runs a number of
beamlines, a number of which are specialized

00:54:52.540 --> 00:55:00.540
for biomedical or biological applications,
crystallography,

00:55:00.540 --> 00:55:03.380
tomography, other methods.

00:55:03.380 --> 00:55:09.110
And then increasingly, they're finding that
they need high performance computing or

00:55:09.110 --> 00:55:18.370
least on-demand computing to enable instantaneous
analysis of data as it is collected rather

00:55:18.370 --> 00:55:24.250
than as was used to
be the case sending the data home on a USB

00:55:24.250 --> 00:55:29.010
drive or I guess floppy disk in the old days.

00:55:29.010 --> 00:55:35.290
And so we're finding that
they need these sorts of computing models

00:55:35.290 --> 00:55:36.670
that I was talking about here.

00:55:36.670 --> 00:55:43.400
And in fact, we're starting to use a mix of
cloud services to manage and dispatch computations

00:55:43.400 --> 00:55:48.210
and then high
performance computing facilities at Argonne

00:55:48.210 --> 00:55:54.420
to actually do the data reduction, the data
analysis, and so forth.

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:59.200
A
number of these increasingly have a sort of

00:55:59.200 --> 00:56:05.050
machine learning component to them, perhaps
for detecting errors or

00:56:05.050 --> 00:56:06.990
labeling data.

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:16.150
And so we're finding that our computing facilities
at Argonne are starting to evolve from things

00:56:16.150 --> 00:56:19.290
that have been
focused almost exclusively on simulation to

00:56:19.290 --> 00:56:24.610
increasingly being used for data analysis,
learning, and so forth.

00:56:24.610 --> 00:56:26.380
And
that's one of the reasons why we recently

00:56:26.380 --> 00:56:30.070
established the Data Science of Learning Division
at Argonne, which

00:56:30.070 --> 00:56:34.090
we're just setting up at the moment and which
will be focused on those sorts of applications.

00:56:34.090 --> 00:56:36.310
Oh, it's great.

00:56:36.310 --> 00:56:41.750
One thing that someone had asked about is
when you were discussing the ENCODE project,

00:56:41.750 --> 00:56:45.900
you
mentioned something about the BDBag and the

00:56:45.900 --> 00:56:47.630
[? baggage ?] methodology.

00:56:47.630 --> 00:56:51.470
Do you care to elaborate a little bit
more on that?

00:56:51.470 --> 00:56:56.810
And is there some easy way to know when you
might want to use that?

00:56:56.810 --> 00:56:58.180
Yeah.

00:56:58.180 --> 00:57:03.460
So this is something that came out of the
BDBag.

00:57:03.460 --> 00:57:09.480
It stands for big data bag and I think-- well,
maybe we

00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:14.300
don't need to look at the slide-- but one
of the slides showed us exchanging data between

00:57:14.300 --> 00:57:19.790
phases of the
computation using this notation.

00:57:19.790 --> 00:57:24.470
It's a set of conventions that originally
came out of the library community, the socalled

00:57:24.470 --> 00:57:27.869
Bagit specification.

00:57:27.869 --> 00:57:39.380
It's a very lightweight set of rules and functions
for bundling up some data and

00:57:39.380 --> 00:57:48.040
associated metadata all in terms of files
in a defined file system structure and using

00:57:48.040 --> 00:57:56.810
JSON format to represent
metadata, checksums, and others such information.

00:57:56.810 --> 00:58:01.940
We developed this in collaboration with people
with USC.

00:58:01.940 --> 00:58:09.230
And we find that whenever we have a computation
that

00:58:09.230 --> 00:58:15.810
involves multiple phases where one phase we
might want to run, produce some data, and

00:58:15.810 --> 00:58:21.420
then perhaps run the
other phase later or on a different computer,

00:58:21.420 --> 00:58:27.480
it works well to underlock the results on
this BDBag format.

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:33.830
And we've got some nice tools that have now
been developed by the community for managing

00:58:33.830 --> 00:58:38.610
these BDBags for
checking the integrity of the data, and using

00:58:38.610 --> 00:58:46.040
the checksums for retrieving elements of a
BDBag when the contents

00:58:46.040 --> 00:58:50.619
are not the actual file but a URL.

00:58:50.619 --> 00:58:58.550
We have galaxy tools for accessing and loading
pipeline tools for processing

00:58:58.550 --> 00:59:02.950
data that are contained in these BDBags and
so on and so forth.

00:59:02.950 --> 00:59:06.720
So, Ian, we're almost at the end of our time
here.

00:59:06.720 --> 00:59:11.180
But I want to give you a minute or two here
just to kind of

00:59:11.180 --> 00:59:16.610
forecast where you see grid and cloud computing
in the next five years.

00:59:16.610 --> 00:59:22.840
Will we be like dispensing with doing local
computing all together and just shipping it

00:59:22.840 --> 00:59:24.340
out to the ether?

00:59:24.340 --> 00:59:27.210
Or do you see any alterations?

00:59:27.210 --> 00:59:32.060
How's it going to
change our lives in the next five years?

00:59:32.060 --> 00:59:35.610
Yeah, I think that's a good question.

00:59:35.610 --> 00:59:42.340
So of course we'll still have academic computing
centers.

00:59:42.340 --> 00:59:46.740
But they will
perhaps be more focused on the very high end

00:59:46.740 --> 00:59:51.740
simulations and other computations that can't
be performed on

00:59:51.740 --> 00:59:53.640
cloud computing platforms.

00:59:53.640 --> 00:59:59.610
I think particularly for smaller institutions,
they need to be looking very hard at where

00:59:59.610 --> 01:00:06.030
does it make sense to compute locally versus
remotely, and looking at doing sort of a full

01:00:06.030 --> 01:00:11.850
cost analysis, and also
trying to understand the trade-offs involved

01:00:11.850 --> 01:00:21.450
in having to maintain and then compete for
time on a overloaded local

01:00:21.450 --> 01:00:27.660
system versus being able to reach out to a
cloud service.

01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:35.550
But then the other area where we hopefully
will see great progress is in the services

01:00:35.550 --> 01:00:39.550
that are operated on cloud
platforms for scientists.

01:00:39.550 --> 01:00:42.830
We're seeing a growing number of these.

01:00:42.830 --> 01:00:46.010
But I think we can go much further.

01:00:46.010 --> 01:00:49.450
Perhaps the
cloud, in one form or another, will hopefully

01:00:49.450 --> 01:00:55.270
be the place where we can process the data
about millions of patients

01:00:55.270 --> 01:01:01.980
that's starting to become available from various
programs in the US and abroad.

01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:06.130
It seems impractical to imagine
every institution setting up the machinery

01:01:06.130 --> 01:01:12.520
required to collect, to create a copy of a
million genomes and analyze

01:01:12.520 --> 01:01:21.032
them, but a central cloud hosted facility
could quite reasonably be used for that purpose.

01:01:21.032 --> 01:01:22.270
Well, we'll look forward to that time.

01:01:22.270 --> 01:01:25.690
But we've reached the end of our time though
today.

01:01:25.690 --> 01:01:30.310
And I'd like to thank Dr.
Ian Foster from the University of Chicago

01:01:30.310 --> 01:01:33.900
and Argonne National Laboratory once again
for telling us why the cloud

01:01:33.900 --> 01:01:35.040
matters for data science.

01:01:35.040 --> 01:01:39.670
And I want to thank everybody who joined us
today for tuning in into The Big Data to

01:01:39.670 --> 01:01:42.790
Knowledge Guide to the Fundamentals of Data
Science.

01:01:42.790 --> 01:01:44.190
Once again, thank you, everyone.

01:01:44.190 --> 01:01:45.710
Thank you, Dr. Foster.

01:01:45.710 --> 01:01:49.740
And everybody have a wonderful extra long
